  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 323 AM EST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nA low is expected to swing across the Gulf for the early \nweek period next week, which will usher in deeper moisture under a \nsouthwesterly flow. Guidance still suggests PWATs rising above \naverage to the 1.8-2.0\" range with vorticity advection occurring \naround the mid-level trough into the region. However, vertical \ngrowth could be tempered by an upper level anticyclone centered over \nthe Caribbean near Cuba that will be circulating over South Florida \nat the same time as the vorticity and moisture advection occurs \naround the base of the mid-level trough.  \n\nAs a result of the counteracting features mentioned above, this \nsetup may not produce steady rainfall until Tuesday when the \nanticyclone gets forced out of the area by the incoming mid-level \ntrough and an upper level jet streak helps increase upper level \ndivergence. Thus, at the moment the expectation is for Monday to \nexperience some scattered shower activity with perhaps an outside \nchance for a thunderstorm, but this would be a highly conditional \nrisk given the expected vertical growth capping caused by the upper \nlevel high. Currently, Tuesday has the best chance for a more \nwidespread rainfall day along with periods of moderate to heavy \nrain. The next frontal boundary is then currently expected to clear \nout the deeper moisture sometime towards the Wed-Thu time frame, \nalthough the strength of this front may not result in moisture being \nfully cleared out as the front washes out in its approach. \nNevertheless, rain chances are expected to drop considerably by the \nmiddle to end of next week as this front pushes through and upper \nlevel ridging begins to rebuild in strength across the eastern US.\n\nTemperatures will continue to increase slightly each day through the \nlong term period, averaging in the low to mid 80s. Parts of SW \nFlorida may reach the upper 80s as the developing ridge and easterly \nresults in warmer temps in that region. Overnight lows also \nincrease, reaching the upper 60s to low 70s.",
